WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.680
- Hi, I'm Joe Sasto.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.590
If you follow me, you
know very well my love

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.570
of Italian cooking and cuisine.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.930
I also love to share my passion for food

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.850
and California with others.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.590
That's why I'm inviting my friend Breanna

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.050
to come see what the
West Coast has to offer.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.720
- Hi, I'm Breanna, and I
cook competition barbecue

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.320
in Houston, Texas.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.520
Now, I'm not super familiar
with Californian cuisine

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.750
because I'm from Texas and I do barbecue.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.530
Now, I'm a little
nervous to head that way,

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:27.730
but for the sake of this video,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.920
I'm gonna get out of my
comfort zone and live,

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.081
cook, and eat like Joe.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.440
I post a lot of food videos.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.600
I'm always looking for new opportunities

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.010
to learn something new about
different types of food

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.140
that maybe I'm not
exposed to all the time.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.770
And I figured what place
better than California

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.670
to check out some new cuisine.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.270
The thing I don't know about California

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.530
is what makes Californian food awesome.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.600
I know there's a lot of fresh ingredients.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.230
There's some great agriculture.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.880
I'm sure there's some
things in this cuisine

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.670
that I've never heard of and never tried,

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.010
and I'm super excited to try them all out.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:04.843
- Hi, Breanna.

00:01:04.843 --> 00:01:06.040
Welcome to San Francisco.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.190
I'm so excited that you're here.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.800
Now, while the city means
something a little different

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.380
to everyone, there's one thing I think

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.300
we can all agree upon:

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.130
It's that the food
scene here is exquisite.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.640
So while you're here visiting,

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.040
please feel free to make
this journey your own,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.610
but I do have a few recommendations.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.120
First stop, head over to Hayes Valley

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.040
and check out Rich Table.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.250
It's a restaurant serving what I think

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:34.083
is the true definition
of Californian cuisine.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.290
- Welcome to Rich Table. I'm Sarah Rich.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.360
We have a menu that changes all the time.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.950
We like to say: We go to the
market, we see what's good,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:44.783
and we cook it.

00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:46.870
We just wanted to send
out some food tonight

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.280
that we think is really
indicative of what we do here

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.870
at Rich Table.
- Okay.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.390
So these are just little
bites to start your meal off.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.200
These are our porcini dusted donuts,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.820
and you dip them into a
raclette cheese sauce.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.040
And then our sardine chips.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.130
So it's a little sardine filet
woven through a potato chip,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.100
and on the side is some
horseradish crème fraîche.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.223
And they're just a perfect
way to start your meal.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.830
- The fish is a little bit salty.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:17.820
The potato chips, nice and crispy,

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.720
but together they create harmony.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.730
- Here at Rich Table we love pastas.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.350
We love to steer a little
away from traditional.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.290
So for example,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.880
this is our tonnarelli with
sea urchin cacio e pepe.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.330
- I love being playful with food.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.360
That is so right up my alley.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.970
I'm all about mixing
things that people say not

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:38.100
to mix together, so I'm on
board and I'm super excited

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.532
to dig in.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.100
Oh my gosh. That looks beautiful.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.080
- Think of back to your Girl Scout days,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.090
and you'll get the flavors
that we're going for.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.960
- This dessert is everything that mint

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.870
and chocolate should be.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.400
It is creamy with the mousse
on top, the ice cream is cold,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.620
the cookie is cooked perfectly,

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.790
and the mint on top just sets it off.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:11.090
This was one of the best
desserts I think I've ever had.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.980
I think one thing that
that meal really taught me

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.880
about California cuisine
is that they're not afraid

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.350
to use fresh ingredients
and cook on the fly.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.000
You know, they're willing
to go to the market,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.490
and at that point in time,
while they're in the market,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.720
they're deciding what
they're going to serve

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:25.950
for dinner that night,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.040
and I love that because
I feel like in Texas,

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.870
we prepare a lot for
these kinds of things,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.950
but in California, they
just do it on the fly.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.420
- Alright, next stop:

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.970
If you're craving some amazing city views,

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.640
I recommend you head over to Twin Peaks.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.030
I like to park and hike
all the way up to the top

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.630
of the hill for the
best views of the city.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:47.393
Though, do be careful. It
does get a little windy.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.010
- With seeing San Francisco
from such a great vantage point

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:57.360
is seeing the differences
between Texas and California.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.280
Both areas are beautiful
and they both have

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.570
so much to offer, but
they're really different

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:02.740
in a lot of ways.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:03.700
You know, all the great things

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.950
about California don't
necessarily outshine

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.380
the things about Texas.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.370
They're just different,

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.070
and it's so fun that I get
to experience both of them.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.980
I'm at the top of Twin Peaks
and it is beautiful up here.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.620
I feel like this video
doesn't do it justice,

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.340
but this view is insane.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.853
Look at that. That is
all of San Francisco.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.930
It's a beautiful city full
of gorgeous architecture,

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.230
and I'm just really
excited to keep exploring.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.010
- Last stop, and probably
my personal favorite.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:36.980
You should head up north and check out

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.790
the Marin Farmers Market.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.870
I go every week and buy all of my fruits

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.800
and vegetables directly from the farmers.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:44.633
While you're there,

00:04:44.633 --> 00:04:46.970
you should also get all the
ingredients that you need

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:50.010
and try making my perfect
pesto recipe to go

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.210
with a flour and water pasta dough.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.260
You're gonna love it.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.730
- So I'm here to buy
some fresh ingredients

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.233
from the farmers market, make
this pasta out of this world.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.090
Thank you so much. Have a wonderful day.

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.160
These herbs are just absolutely beautiful.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.373
I can smell this basil all day long.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.703
They just smell amazing.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.540
So I just got back from the farmers market

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.390
and I'm ready to make my fresh pasta.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.130
So I've definitely watched Joe's videos

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:25.210
on the internet and he makes
this look so much easier

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:26.800
than it actually is.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.940
You know, sometimes it takes
actually doing something

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.980
with your own hands to realize how much

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:34.730
of an art form certain
styles of cooking are.

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:48.730
It definitely looks like pasta,

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:50.050
but I'm still a little worried

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.300
'cause this is not
something I do very often.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.343
Mm-mm.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.630
I was a little bit
skeptical about adding honey

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:05.120
to something so savory, but
this was such a great idea.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.100
It's got a little kick of sweetness.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.570
It's definitely got that
crunch from the pistachios,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.050
and the basil and the mint

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.450
and the parsley just sing together.

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:15.520
I mean, this is fantastic.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.880
I can tell I'm in California
because the herbs are

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:18.713
so fresh.

00:06:18.713 --> 00:06:20.540
You know we got them
at the farmers market,

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:22.940
and they are just absolutely
the freshest you can get,

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.470
and I swear that makes this dish.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.900
Californian and Texan
cuisine are very different.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.370
I think they both have attributes

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.260
that are just absolutely amazing.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.990
You know, good food is universal.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.530
And I'm so glad that I got
to have this experience

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.220
and that I got to share
it with all of you.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:38.440
- Thanks for visiting, Breanna.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.660
It seems like you learned
a lot about my home

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.580
and my cuisine while you were here.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:45.110
I hope you enjoyed your
journey through this food

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:46.560
and fog-filled city.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.727
Please come back any time.

00:06:54.502 --> 00:06:56.270
- Hi, Chanel. Welcome to LA.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:57.620
- I can't wait for you to check out some

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.874
of my favorite go-to spots.

